 # Scene Representation and Geometry Constraints
 
-Also called: Gaussian Representation and Truncation, Mesh Extraction and Representation
 
 ## Overview
-This family manages the fundamental data structures of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) primitives and the conversion of these volumetric representations into explicit geometric surfaces. It solves the problem of defining the discrete, differentiable primitives used for rasterization and extracting compatible triangular meshes for downstream applications. It sets apart from siblings by providing the core mathematical definition of the Gaussian kernel and the density-based surface reconstruction pipeline, whereas siblings like [Discontinuity-aware Gaussian Splatting](13138-discontinuity-aware-gaussian-splatting.md) or [SuGaR Mesh Extraction](12580-sugar-mesh-extraction.md) modify these definitions to handle specific artifacts or integration constraints.
+This family defines the fundamental data structures and geometric constraints used to represent scenes in 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) and its variants. It addresses the problem of defining learnable primitives and ensuring their geometric consistency, particularly when integrating explicit mesh structures. It differs from sibling methods by focusing on the core mathematical definition of the Gaussian primitives and the constraints applied to their physical parameters, whereas siblings like [Sorting and Rendering Pipeline Optimization](12180-sorting-and-rendering-pipeline-optimization.md) focus on rendering efficiency, and [Mesh Extraction and Representation](14095-mesh-extraction-and-representation.md) focuses on post-processing or surface alignment strategies.
 
